   401	## 8. Content hashes
   402	
   403	The Curator content hash is calculated over regular files excluding the marker
   404	itself. For each selected file in sorted protocol-path order, append:
   405	
   406	```text
   407	UTF8(path) || 0x00 || file_bytes
   408	```
   409	
   410	Join adjacent records with one additional `0x00`, hash the resulting byte
   411	string with SHA-256, encode lowercase hexadecimal, and prefix `sha256:`. The
   412	empty tree hashes the empty byte string. File mode, owner, timestamp, and
   413	filesystem-native separator are not hashed. Readers MUST reject duplicate
   414	protocol paths rather than hash one arbitrarily.
   415	
   416	### 8.1 Build-source identity
   417	
   418	The installed-tree `content_sha256` above remains unchanged and excludes root
   419	`.csk-install.json`. It MUST NOT be used as compiled-artifact identity.
   420	Compiled commands instead bind the fully validated immutable raw snapshot with
   421	algorithm identifier `curator-build-source-v1`, including every regular file
   422	and any package-provided root `.csk-install.json`.
   423	
   424	Snapshot validation MUST occur before build-cache lookup or any Go command.
   425	The snapshot MUST contain only directories and regular files; links, special
   426	files, invalid protocol paths, duplicate encoded paths, and platform path
   427	collisions are invalid. For every regular file, convert its relative protocol
   428	path to `/` separators without normalization and encode its Unicode scalar
   429	values as UTF-8. Sort those path bytes in unsigned bytewise order. Initialize
   430	SHA-256 with exact ASCII `curator-build-source-v1` followed by `0x00`, then
   431	append for each file:
   432	
   433	```text
   434	ASCII("F") || uint64be(path_byte_length) || path_utf8 ||
   435	uint64be(content_byte_length) || file_bytes
   436	```
   437	
   438	Prefix the lowercase digest with `sha256:`. An empty snapshot hashes only the
   439	domain prefix. Modes, ownership, timestamps, ACLs, and extended attributes are
   440	not inputs. Implementations MUST NOT substitute the marker-excluding content
   441	hash with an empty exclusion list: `curator-build-source-v1` is domain-separated
   442	and length-framed. The validated snapshot instance MUST remain byte-for-byte
   443	unchanged until the last build child exits.
   444	
   445	### 8.2 Go toolchain identity
   446	
   447	Every `go-v1` cache input MUST identify its operator-trusted toolchain with
   448	algorithm `curator-go-toolchain-v1`. Resolve the launcher independently of the
   449	package and before entering a package-controlled directory. It MAY be bundled
   450	with the manager or selected by trusted operator configuration, but MUST NOT
   451	come from the repository, a runtime root, project `.agents/bin`, the user
   452	`PATH`, or a manifest. Require it to be the regular executable
   453	`<resolved-goroot>/bin/go` (`bin/go.exe` on Windows), not a wrapper or an
   454	executable outside the tree being fingerprinted. A clean `go env GOROOT` probe
   455	MUST resolve to the same root.
   456	
   457	Walk `GOROOT` without following links; the root itself is not a record.
   458	Relative components MUST contain valid
   459	Unicode scalar values; encode `/`-joined paths as UTF-8 without case folding or
   460	normalization and reject duplicate encoded paths and special files. Symlinks
   461	MUST be relative, non-dangling, and resolve within `GOROOT`, and their referents
   462	have independent tree records. Sort path bytes in unsigned bytewise order.
   463	Initialize SHA-256 with exact ASCII
   464	`curator-go-toolchain-v1` followed by `0x00`, then append for every entry:
   465	
   466	```text
   467	kind || uint64be(path_byte_length) || path_utf8 ||
   468	uint64be(payload_byte_length) || payload
   469	```
   470	
   471	`kind` is ASCII `D`, `F`, or `L`; directory payload is empty, file payload is
   472	the exact bytes, and link payload is the exact UTF-8 `readlink` value. Hard
   473	links are independent regular-file records. Normalize `go version` stdout by
   474	requiring at most 4096 bytes and exactly one terminal LF, optionally preceded
   475	by CR, removing that terminator, and rejecting any other CR, LF, NUL, empty, or
   476	invalid UTF-8 content. Append it as one final `V` record with an empty path.
   477	Prefix the lowercase digest with `sha256:`.
   478	
   479	Permissions, ownership, timestamps, ACLs, and extended attributes are not hash
   480	inputs, but `bin/go` and each invoked `pkg/tool/<host>/` child MUST be regular
   481	and executable at use time. The tree MUST remain unchanged through the last
   482	child exit. The logical identity also records normalized `go_version` and
   483	`go_relpath: "bin/go"`; toolchain location is not portable identity.
   484	
   485	## 9. Compiled-artifact cache and receipts
   486	
   487	For each active build command, the manager MUST construct a logical build-input
   488	object containing schema version 1, driver, the `curator-build-source-v1`
   489	identity, `build_root`, command name, `source_dir`, native GOOS/GOARCH/tuning,
   490	the complete `curator-go-toolchain-v1` identity, and these fixed policy values:
   491	
   492	```json
   493	{
   494	  "module_mode": "vendor",
   495	  "network": "none",
   496	  "workspace": false,
   497	  "cgo": false,
   498	  "compiler_directives": "reject-nonstandard-cgo-import-dynamic-v1",
   499	  "target_mode": "native",
   500	  "link_mode": "internal",
   501	  "libgcc": "none",
   502	  "package_assembly": false,
   503	  "host_objects": false,
   504	  "telemetry": "off-private"
   505	}
   506	```
   507	
   508	The logical cache key is:
   509	
   510	```text
   511	"sha256:" || lowercase_hex(SHA-256(CCJ-1(build_input)))
   512	```
   513	
   514	`CCJ-1` is defined in `registry.md`; the input bytes have no BOM,
   515	insignificant whitespace, or terminal LF. A semantic change to arguments,
   516	environment, sandbox-relevant policy, receipt interpretation, or output rules
   517	requires a new driver identifier or an explicit versioned cache-key policy.
   518	
   519	Each immutable logical entry contains exactly one manager-derived artifact and
   520	a strict build-receipt schema 1. The receipt contains its schema version,
   521	logical cache key, the complete build input, and artifact-relative path,
   522	SHA-256, and byte length. Stored receipt bytes MUST equal `CCJ-1(receipt)`
   523	exactly. Define:
   524	
   525	```text
   526	receipt_sha256 = "sha256:" ||
   527	  lowercase_hex(SHA-256(exact_stored_receipt_bytes))
   528	```
   529	
   530	Before reuse, a reader MUST open a regular singly linked receipt without
   531	following links, recanonicalize it, and require exact stored-byte equality;
   532	recompute the build-source identity before opening the candidate; recompute and
   533	match the cache key and entire expected input; require the manager-derived
   534	artifact path; and open, bound, hash, and size-check one regular singly linked
   535	artifact without following links. It MUST reject unknown receipt fields or
   536	unsupported receipt, driver, build-source, and toolchain versions. It MUST NOT
   537	execute the artifact.
   538	
   539	Persistent reuse is permitted only below manager-created, manager-protected
   540	state resolved independently of package input. On every lookup and again under
   541	the manager-home mutation lock, the manager MUST verify ownership, private
   542	mutation permissions or DACL, containment, regular file types, and link safety
   543	for the boundary and every entry component. An implementation that cannot
   544	prove this boundary MUST disable persistent reuse. A real operation rebuilds
   545	from the revalidated snapshot into newly established protected state; dry-run
   546	reports `would-rebuild-untrusted-cache`; status is non-current. Self-consistent
   547	receipt, marker, artifact, and hash bytes do not repair or authenticate an
   548	untrusted boundary.
   549	
   550	The logical cache key, exact receipt bytes, artifact-relative path, artifact
   551	bytes/hash/size, and validation outcomes are portable. Manager-home paths,
   552	physical cache-root and driver-directory names, receipt filenames, lock and
   553	quarantine names, and storage backends are implementation-specific. A manager
   554	MUST NOT infer portable paths such as `build-cache` or `.csk-build.json`.
   555	
   556	Entries are built in operation-private staging and published atomically and
   557	immutably under the manager-home mutation lock. Receipt hashes are deterministic
   558	corruption/currentness identifiers, not signatures, MACs, attestations, or
   559	proof of provenance. The verified protected-state boundary supplies local
   560	provenance; registry audit continues to attest source, not the compiled result.
   561	
   562	Dry-run MAY validate the raw snapshot and build-root exclusion, compute build
   563	source and trusted-toolchain identity, and inspect protected entries read-only.
   564	It MUST NOT invoke `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or linker; create Go
   565	caches, a persistent memo, a mutation lock or journal; publish, quarantine,
   566	repair, or adopt cache bytes; copy build roots into runtime; or mutate
   567	installation, registry, audit, marker, shim, adapter, or consumer state. A
   568	cache hit MUST use the same static context exclusion and, after exact receipt
   569	validation, MUST NOT run source-aware Go commands.
   570	
   571	## 10. Install markers
   572	
   573	Every installed closure node has `.csk-install.json`. Managers MUST read marker
   574	schemas 1 and 2, MUST write schema 2 on every installation mutation, and MAY
   575	continue to regard a valid marker-v1 installation as current for a schema 1
   576	through 5 package. Marker v1 retains its existing shape and meaning.
   577	
   578	Marker v2 permits `skill_schema_version` through 6 and requires sorted
   579	`build_roots` and a `builds` object, including empty values for installations
   580	without active compiled commands. `build_source` is REQUIRED exactly when
   581	`builds` is non-empty and MUST otherwise be absent. Each lexically ordered
   582	`builds` entry records driver, logical cache key, `receipt_sha256`, artifact
   583	SHA-256, and manager-derived artifact-relative path. A build shim MUST target
   584	the immutable cache artifact selected by the effective plan and marker, not the
   585	commit-keyed script runtime store.
   586	
   587	`locale` is always present and is a string or `null`. Required set-like arrays
   588	are always arrays, including when empty.
   589	
   590	The following arrays are set-like and writers sort them by Unicode scalar
   591	value: `agents`, `commands`, `dependencies`, `files`, `requirements`,
   592	`runtime_roots`, `build_roots`, `requirers`, `activation.commands`, and every
   593	MCP agent list. Writers sort `builds` keys by Unicode scalar value. Object
   594	member order and whitespace are not significant.
   595	
   596	An installation is current only when the marker schema is supported; ref kind,
   597	ref, commit, locale, agents, activation, substitution, MCP findings, and
   598	attestation match the effective plan; and the installed content hash matches
   599	`content_sha256`. For a build-enabled marker, currentness additionally requires
   600	the declared build roots and static context exclusion to match; the fully
   601	validated raw snapshot's `build_source` to match the effective plan and every
   602	receipt input; each logical cache key and manager-derived artifact path to
   603	match; and every referenced receipt/artifact to validate below a currently
   604	verified manager-protected boundary. A missing raw snapshot, prompt-visible or
   605	runtime-copied build-root file, untrusted boundary, corrupt receipt/artifact,
   606	wrong target or toolchain, or mismatched source identity makes the installation
   607	non-current. Repair MUST rebuild from a revalidated snapshot into protected
   608	state rather than adopt candidate bytes.
   609	
   610	Unsupported or unreadable markers are not current. A moved tag is a warning,
   611	or an error under strict-tag policy.
   612	
   613	The marker and its referenced receipt hashes are not signatures and MUST NOT be
   614	used as authorization tokens or provenance proof. Registry attestations are
   615	reverified from signed records when fresh trust is required.
   616	
   617	## 11. Adapter ledger
   618	
   619	Every managed adapter root contains `.csk-managed.json` conforming to
   620	`adapter-ledger-v1.schema.json`. `entries` is a sorted unique list of skill
     1	# Decision 0004: compile-only build drivers
     2	
     3	## Context
     4	
     5	Schemas 1 through 5 can copy script commands or resolve system commands but
     6	cannot produce a native artifact from source. Allowing a package to supply a
     7	shell command, executable, argument vector, environment, plugin, generator, or
     8	build-system recipe would contradict the existing rule that installation does
     9	not execute package-provided code.
    10	
    11	Compiled artifacts also cannot reuse the existing `skill + commit` runtime
    12	identity. Compiler-visible inputs include files outside one package directory,
    13	the selected native target and policy, and the exact trusted toolchain. An
    14	internally consistent receipt from an attacker-writable cache is not proof that
    15	the manager produced its artifact.
    16	
    17	## Decision
    18	
    19	Manifest schema 6 is a declarative compiled-artifact extension. It adds
    20	explicit `build_roots` and one closed command object containing only
    21	`type: "build"`, `driver: "go-v1"`, and `source_dir`. Build roots are dedicated
    22	link-free module roots below the snapshot, cannot be `.`, cannot overlap
    23	runtime roots, and are statically excluded from agent context and runtime
    24	copying before cache lookup or compiler discovery. The same exclusion applies
    25	to real builds, exact cache hits, and dry-runs. Artifacts live only in
    26	manager-owned staging and immutable cache state.
    27	
    28	Protocol v1 defines only `go-v1`. It is a fixed native, vendor-only,
    29	networkless, cgo-free Go 1.23+ pipeline using an operator-trusted and
    30	fingerprinted release family. The manager owns the process graph, environment,
    31	arguments, target, output path, dependency preflight, directive checks, and
    32	internal-link policy. It rejects non-standard assembly, host objects,
    33	`//go:cgo_import_dynamic`, external linking, libgcc fallback, PGO, workspaces,
    34	toolchain switching, and any executable child outside the fingerprinted Go
    35	tool directory. The built artifact is never executed during manager activity.
    36	
    37	Two source/currentness identities remain separate:
    38	
    39	- installed-tree `content_sha256` retains its schema 1 through 5 semantics and
    40	  excludes the manager marker;
    41	- `curator-build-source-v1` is domain-separated and length-framed over every
    42	  regular file in the validated raw snapshot, including a package-provided
    43	  root `.csk-install.json`, and is computed before cache lookup.
    44	
    45	The build input also binds `curator-go-toolchain-v1`, command/source selection,
    46	native target and tuning, driver revision, and the complete fixed policy. Its
    47	CCJ-1 digest is the logical cache key. Exact canonical receipt bytes bind the
    48	input and manager-derived artifact path/hash/size; their digest supplies
    49	corruption and currentness detection, not authentication. Persistent reuse
    50	requires a separately verified manager-created, owner-protected, link-safe
    51	cache boundary. Untrusted protection forces a rebuild from the revalidated
    52	snapshot, even when every embedded hash matches.
    53	
    54	Marker schema 2 records build roots, build-source identity, logical cache keys,
    55	receipt hashes, artifact hashes, and manager-derived artifact-relative paths.
    56	Managers read marker schemas 1 and 2, write v2 after mutation, and may retain a
    57	valid v1 marker as current for schema 1 through 5 packages. Shims for build
    58	commands select immutable cache artifacts rather than the commit-keyed script
    59	runtime store.
    60	
    61	A real operation validates and builds misses in operation-private staging
    62	before installation mutation. Independent projects may validate and compile
    63	concurrently, but cache publication, project/global/hybrid/adapter mutation,
    64	consumer update, rollback/recovery, and garbage collection serialize beneath
    65	one manager-home mutation lock. One journal covers the ordered mutable target
    66	set; the consumer ledger commits last; rollback stays locked and proceeds in
    67	reverse commit order. This prevents cross-project lost updates and stale
    68	backup restoration.
    69	
    70	Dry-run performs only package-independent toolchain probes and read-only
    71	planning. It does not run `go list`, `go build`, a compiler, or linker; create
    72	Go caches, persistent fingerprint memos, mutation locks, journals, or manager
    73	state; publish/quarantine/repair cache entries; or change agent context,
    74	runtime, markers, shims, adapters, audit/registry state, or consumers.
    75	
    76	Only logical identity and validation are portable. Manager-home directories,
    77	physical cache-root and driver-subdirectory names, receipt filenames, lock and
    78	quarantine names, and storage backends are deliberately non-normative. An
    79	illustrative layout such as `<home>/<cache>/go-v1/<key>/bin/<command>` MUST NOT
    80	be treated as a compatibility identifier.
    81	
    82	## Rejected alternatives
    83	
    84	- Arbitrary package shell commands, executable names, argv/environment arrays,
    85	  output selection, hooks, plugins, generators, tests, and pre/post-build steps
    86	  were rejected because they transfer install-time execution control to the
    87	  package.
    88	- Cargo was rejected for v1 because build scripts and procedural macros execute
    89	  package-selected code. Direct Rust compilation still needs a closed
    90	  dependency and linking contract.
    91	- Zig and Swift were deferred because their build manifests, plugins, macros,
    92	  or compile-time execution require distinct process and side-effect models.
    93	- Make, CMake, Ninja, and Meson were rejected as package-controlled recipe
    94	  frontends rather than fixed compilers.
    95	- Maven, Gradle, MSBuild, and package-oriented Java/Kotlin/.NET compilation were
    96	  deferred because tasks, plugins, annotation processors, analyzers, and source
    97	  generators execute at build time.
    98	- npm lifecycle scripts and Node/TypeScript bundler configuration, loaders, and
    99	  plugins were rejected. A fixed transpiler or Deno subset still needs a closed
   100	  dependency, configuration, and runtime-output model.
   101	- Python package builds were rejected because PEP 517 frontends execute the
   102	  package-selected backend and may resolve dynamic build requirements.
   103	- Direct C/C++ compilers were deferred because a portable driver must own and
   104	  identify source enumeration, preprocessing, sysroots, response files,
   105	  compiler plugins, native dependencies, and linking inputs.
   106	- `skill + commit`, installed-tree `content_sha256`, and receipt-only trust were
   107	  rejected as cache identities because they omit compiler/toolchain policy or
   108	  allow attacker-created self-consistent candidates.
   109	- Standardizing cache directory names and receipt filenames was rejected
   110	  because machine-local layout is an implementation boundary, not wire state.
   111	
   112	## Compatibility impact
   113	
   114	Schema 1 through 5 manifests and marker-v1 currentness retain their exact
   115	deployed behavior. They reject the schema-6-only field and command surface.
   116	Schema 6 and marker v2 are explicit reader/writer version transitions; readers
   117	never infer them from fields. The build feature is released with a new current
   118	protocol `1.0.0-rc.4` conformance claim schema 2. Conformance claim schema 1
   119	remains frozen as historical rc.3 evidence and cannot claim schema-6 or
   120	build-driver behavior.
   121	
   122	## Security impact
   123	
   124	The accepted model preserves the no-hooks invariant while admitting untrusted
   125	compiler input. It does not claim that the Go compiler or compiled artifact is
   126	safe. Compiler-input denial of service and vulnerabilities require host
   127	resource limits and sandboxing, while artifact execution remains a later user
   128	action. Protected local cache state is part of the v1 trusted computing base;
   129	same-principal, administrator/root, kernel, and hostile-storage compromise are
   130	outside that boundary and require cache reuse to be disabled when unacceptable.
   131	
   132	## Future-driver rule
   133	
   134	A future driver requires a new closed identifier and independent protocol and
   135	security review. The proposal must define a strict manifest surface, complete
   136	compiler-input boundary, trusted toolchain identity, fixed direct process graph
   137	and environment, network/dependency/link policy, manager-derived output,
   138	logical cache/receipt identity, dry-run behavior, lifecycle/rollback rules, and
   139	cross-language conformance vectors. It MUST demonstrate that package-selected
   140	hooks, plugins, generators, macros, build recipes, and produced output cannot
   141	execute during manager activity. No future driver may be enabled by broadening
   142	`go-v1` or adding a generic fallback.

